# SMS Spam Filter – Breaking the Model

## Project Overview

This project builds a simple SMS spam classifier using **Machine Learning**. It uses TF-IDF to convert text messages into numerical features and the Multinomial Naive Bayes algorithm to classify messages as either spam or ham (not spam).

The goal is not only to train the model but also to test its weaknesses by creating messages that may fool the classifier.

## Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas
- Scikit-learn
- TF-IDF Vectorizer
- Multinomial Naive Bayes
- Jupyter Notebook

## Dataset

The project uses the **SMS Spam Collection Dataset**, which contains SMS messages labeled as:

- `spam` – Unwanted or promotional messages.
- `ham` – Normal, legitimate messages.

Dataset source: [UCI SMS Spam Collection](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/228/sms+spam+collection)

## How the Model Works

1. Load the SMS dataset.
2. Split the dataset into training and testing sets (80% training and 20% testing).
3. Use TF-IDF to convert text messages into numerical features.
4. Train a Multinomial Naive Bayes classifier.
5. Predict whether test messages are spam or ham.
6. Calculate the model's accuracy.
7. Test the model using specially written messages to identify its weaknesses.

## Model Accuracy

The model was evaluated using accuracy score.

| Evaluation | Result |
|---|---|
| Training data | 80% |
| Testing data | 20% |
| Model accuracy | [0.9668161434977578] |

## What I Learned

1. Machine learning models can achieve high accuracy but still make mistakes.
2. TF-IDF converts text into numerical features that machine learning algorithms can process.
3. Naive Bayes can struggle with spelling variations, negation, and context.
4. Testing a model with unusual examples helps reveal weaknesses that accuracy alone cannot show.
5. A high accuracy score does not mean a model is perfect.

